# What the build context must not carry.
#
# The Dockerfile copies named folders rather than `COPY . .`, so most of this
# is about keeping the context small and fast to send. The secret rules are
# the exception and are load bearing: they keep .env files out of any layer
# even if a later edit copies more than it should. A file that lands in a
# layer stays readable to anyone who can pull the image, whatever a later
# stage deletes.
#
# Secret rules are written `**/name`, not `name`. Docker matches patterns
# against the whole path from the context root and `*` does not cross a
# slash, so a bare `.env` would only exclude the file at the root.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/.envrc
**/*.pem
**/*.key

# History. Both hold every past revision of every file.
.git
.jj

# Rebuilt inside the image. The host copies are the wrong platform anyway:
# sharp and argon2 ship native binaries per OS and CPU.
**/node_modules
.pnpm-store
dist
*.tsbuildinfo

# Local data a development server wrote: uploaded images and room exports.
.data

# Tests never run in the image, and the runtime stage copies server/ and
# shared/ whole, so leaving them out here keeps them out of production.
**/*.test.ts
**/*.itest.ts
e2e
test-results
playwright-report

# Not part of the application. docker/ holds the egress proxy's config, which
# is bind mounted into the squid container and has no business in this image.
docs
screenshots
docker
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
README.md
.claude
.vscode
.idea
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store
